#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064014 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06683b40-3991-5149-8a0a-86a938d43b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296135} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3bf19e-e0ab-5cb9-90b1-f43021992e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296136} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0c7cee-5881-5103-bd91-3df14f29b289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296137} {:answer/id #uuid "98a5a131-b799-5b3b-a0a3-c57bf567eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} {:answer/id #uuid "de7379ac-edf1-5569-8713-27d0beec1603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98a5a131-b799-5b3b-a0a3-c57bf567eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1f923a-4ff1-53c6-af71-81359ee48ab3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fa09994-b03e-519d-aa09-462602c3715d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114032" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $3{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161035dd-534e-5c12-88df-5fe26ee6a1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296142} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f91561-2994-50d4-8899-964b34007fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296143} {:answer/id #uuid "6870e8fd-8f88-5aed-8b88-d157b85205d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296144} {:answer/id #uuid "79dd410d-7feb-59d2-8793-065ac1904021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296145} {:answer/id #uuid "966fc9a3-c000-5205-81d7-a858502c46c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79dd410d-7feb-59d2-8793-065ac1904021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296145} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b5e482-b9e5-5c06-9080-0da05e7a456c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "229d26fc-7842-5b85-86ac-4396d46bdbad" :question/math-academy-id "q-114045" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{16\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/16)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/16}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41586ce1-c84e-52f5-afa7-4a8d0c32f0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296149} {:answer/id #uuid "d8377633-bacf-5c7c-b510-899fe804aff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296150} {:answer/id #uuid "74c30c11-17d0-5fed-aef3-6138bd144eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296151} {:answer/id #uuid "ea14f68c-bd33-5965-bba7-bb3e19cfd9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296152} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd4ad11-4f50-547b-a2a7-ac496d7c777a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd4ad11-4f50-547b-a2a7-ac496d7c777a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296153} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc0e267-f80c-5ee2-a278-454fed19ebbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc2c1d59-0a7c-5002-a982-86e3a36461a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-114053" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{|\\sqrt{3}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{3}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{|\\sqrt{3}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
